    "lawCode": "Amd §§152 & 153, Ec Dev L",
    "lawSection": "Economic Development Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9268\n\nSPONSOR: RYAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the economic development law, in relation to the\ncreation of an \"I Love NY Historic Small Business\" niche brand\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nDirects Empire State Development to include the niche brand \"I Love NY\nHistoric Small Business\" in its marketing guidelines for the \"I Love NY\"\nbrand to highlight historic small businesses listed on the State's\nregistry of historic businesses.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: amends subdivisions 1 and 5 of Economic Development Law § 152\nto include the niche brand \"I Love NY Historic Small Business\" in the\ndepartment's marketing guidelines for the brand \"I Love NY\" and in its\nannual reporting on the department's activities related to the brand.\n\nSection 2: amends subdivision 1 of Economic Development Law § 153 to\nauthorize eligible entities to market multiple, thematically related\nassets or activities in a single marketing campaign, including in\nrelation to the \"I Love NY Historic Small Business\" niche brand.\n\nSection 3: effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe majority of businesses in New York State are small businesses, many\nof which reflect and enhance the rich history and traditions of their\nlocal communities. New York's registry of historic businesses celebrates\nunique and iconic business that have operated in New York communities\nfor 50 years or longer (with no more than a two-year break) and provides\na ready-resource of historic tourist attractions. This bill capitalizes\non the durability, success, and ingenuity of New York's historic small\nbusinesses by celebrating them as a niche component of the state's long-\nstanding \"I Love NY\" campaign. By directing ESD to include \"I Love NY\nHistoric Small Business\" in its marketing guidance and grant eligibil-\nity, the state will help ensure the survival of these long-standing\nbusinesses in the future.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
